            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The Church, which possesses no innate light but reflects the divine light of Christ (the Sun of Righteousness), often associated with phases of waxing and waning through earthly history and persecution.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Song of Solomon 6:10\",\"Genesis 1:16\",\"Psalm 89:37\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Cosmic judgment, apocalyptic upheaval, and the Day of the Lord (specifically when darkening or turning to blood).\",\"basis\":[\"Joel 2:31\",\"Acts 2:20\",\"Revelation 6:12\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The Virgin Mary, symbolizing purity, spiritual illumination, and triumph over temporal mutability.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 12:1\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The passage of time, natural cycles, seasons, and the divinely ordained order of the cosmos.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:14\",\"Psalm 104:19\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Pagan idolatry, false worship, or spiritual blindness when treated as a deity.\",\"basis\":[\"Deuteronomy 4:19\",\"Job 31:26-28\",\"Jeremiah 8:2\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Interpretation of the moon is highly context-dependent in Christian tradition. Early patristic writers frequently invoked the 'mysterium lunae' (mystery of the moon) as a primary ecclesiological metaphor for the Church reflecting Christ's light. In apocalyptic biblical texts, lunar anomalies universally signal divine judgment and the disruption of the cosmos. In Catholic and Orthodox iconography, Revelation 12:1 has firmly associated the moon with Marian symbolism.\"}",
